    {
      "id": "30ae4352-08ed-4332-b840-2a19d4376aa7",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## What the record book says\n\n25 completed editions (2001/02 → 2025/26) plus four sparse StatsBomb-era\nseasons shown as partial coverage.\n\n- **Real Madrid are the kings of the modern era**: 7 titles in the window\n  (2002, 2014, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2022, 2024) — more than any other club.\n  Barcelona (4), Bayern Munich, Chelsea, Liverpool and Milan (2 each) and\n  PSG (2) complete the list; Inter's 2010 treble is the only other title.\n- **The greatest champion season**: Bayern Munich 2019/20 went **11W-0D**\n  (43 scored, 8 conceded) — the best goal difference (+35) and the most\n  dominant run in the dataset, capped by the Lisbon final.\n- **Deadliest champions**: PSG 2025/26 scored 45 goals en route to the\n  title — the most by any champion. Chelsea 2020/21 were the stingiest\n  (4 conceded).\n- **Unbeaten champions**: five editions were won without a single defeat —\n  Barcelona 2006, Man United 2008, Bayern 2020, Man City 2023, Real Madrid\n  2024.\n- **Penalties decide everything tight**: six of the eight closest finals\n  went to a shootout — including back-to-back PSG penalty triumphs in\n  2025 and 2026.\n- **Biggest win**: HJK Helsinki 10–0 Bangor City (2011/12 qualifying) —\n  the largest margin in Champions League history.\n\n*Source: ESPN import via `dev.db`; player-level stats too sparse for a\ntop-scorers table.*"
    }
  ]
}